BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Art of Community Festival - Art of Community Awards
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260809T120000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260809T160000
UID:260728etEnETpilk61xxEBLd2yUQ
DESCRIPTION:We are proud to present the 3rd Annual Art of Community Festiv
 al\, a month-long celebration of creativity\, culture\, and community taki
 ng place throughout August on the historic Mare Island waterfront.\n\nWhat
  began as a grassroots community art exhibition has grown into a dynamic f
 estival that brings together artists\, youth creatives\, makers\, cultural
  organizations\, and community members in a shared celebration of the peop
 le and ideas that make Vallejo unique.\n\nBay Area artist Diana Krevsky's 
 exhibit "Legacy in Color" in the main gallery will be open all Sundays in 
 August. Krevsky’s work often explores the conflict between cultural valu
 es and individual identity within both political and natural landscapes. H
 er paintings and sculptures are at once colorful\, whimsical\, thought-pro
 voking\, and deeply personal. Visitors can meet the artist during the fest
 ival’s Opening Reception and Artist Talk Aug 8 at 2 p.m.\n\nAug 9\, 2 to
  4 p.m. Art of Community Awards Honoring individuals and organizations mak
 ing a meaningful impact through art\, culture\, and community.\n\nAug 16\,
  Mare Island Art • Fashion • Vintage Bazaar A waterfront market featur
 ing artists\, makers\, fashion vendors\, vintage dealers\, collectors\, an
 d creative entrepreneurs.\n\nAug 23\, Vallejo Youth Artists “Under 21”
  Exhibition Reception & Poetry Showcase Featuring emerging artists and you
 ng creative voices from throughout the region.
LOCATION:Mare Island Art Studios
URL:https://mareislandartstudios.com/events/art-of-community-festival-2026
 /
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:A Revolutionary Afternoon
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260809T133000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260809T160000
UID:260728ZjkPlZ2mDEib59Q-ZBkxbw
DESCRIPTION:An America 250! Tea featuring Dr. Emily Sneff\, author of Wh
 en the Declaration of Independence Was News.\n\nJoin us for an elegant aft
 ernoon of tea and history as we welcome Dr. Emily Sneff\, an early Americ
 an historian and nationally recognized expert on the Declaration of Indepe
 ndence.\n\nGuests will enjoy sweet and savory tea fare\, beautifully appo
 inted tables\, and a fascinating presentation exploring how news of Ameri
 can independence spread throughout the colonies and beyond.\n\nAbout Dr. S
 neff: Joining us from the East Coast\, Dr. Sneff is an early American hist
 orian\, author of When the Declaration of Independence Was News\, and con
 sulting curator for America 250 exhibitions\, including projects with Phil
 adelphia's Museum of the American Revolution. Her research explores how ne
 ws of American independence spread throughout the colonies and across the 
 Atlantic in 1776. www.emilysneff.com
LOCATION:The Saturday Afternoon Club
URL:https://thesaturdayafternoonclub.com/events
X-SOURCE:North Bay Bohemian
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:Then & Now: Faces of the Valley Reception and Film Screening
DTSTART;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260809T160000
DTEND;TZID=America/Los_Angeles:20260809T193000
UID:260728ZGAntJmgeUepqVOHmhVTfg
DESCRIPTION:This August\, the San Geronimo Valley Community Center in part
 nership with Marin AgeWell Collaborative and Two Valley Community Land Tru
 st a thoughtful series of exhibitions and events exploring aging\, memory\
 , housing\, and the meaning of growing older in community. Centered around
  the traveling exhibit What About Age\, recently shown at the Marin County
  Civic Center\, the series invites residents to reflect on the joys\, chal
 lenges\, and deep connections that come with aging in the San Geronimo and
  Nicasio Valleys.\n\nA special feature of the exhibit will be a companion 
 “Then & Now: Faces of the Valley” photography installation pairing vin
 tage Polaroids taken by the late local photographer Chuck Ford during the 
 Community Center’s Holiday Arts Faire in the 1970s and 1980s with contem
 porary portraits by acclaimed artist photographer Sasha Gulish. Together\,
  the images create an intimate portrait of community members across genera
 tions\, capturing both continuity and change over time. Through photograph
 s\, stories\, and personal reflections\, dozens of local residents share w
 hat it has meant to grow older in this uniquely beautiful and interconnect
 ed rural community.\n\nThe show will open with a reception on Sunday\, Aug
 ust 9th at 4pm and will include a screening of the highly regarded PBS doc
 umentary Lives Well Lived\, a moving film celebrating the wisdom and exper
 iences of adults aged 75 to 100.
LOCATION:San Geronimo Valley Community Center
